      "thinking": "The user gives me part 4 of 4 of a certificate fragment — it's the marking/inscriptions section, security measures, and figures of an OIML R49 water meter certificate (R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3). I'm told to produce a JSON object conforming to the layered schema for content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections not present.\n\nWhat's actually present in this fragment:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3 (appears in headers)\n- Page count: 13 pages total (Page 10 of 13, Page 11 of 13, Page 12 of 13, Page 13 of 13)\n- Section 4: Marking and inscriptions — a list of required markings\n- Section 5: Security measures — sealing description\n- Section 6: Figures — images of water meters type QALCOSONIC W1, designs A and B\n- Faceplate markings observed: \"CE\", \"M21\", \"T30\", \"QM15\", \"R160\", \"AXIOMA\" name\n- Sealing drawings for DN15/DN20, DN25/DN32, DN40\n\nThe schema has certificate fields: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id.\n\nFrom the fragment I can extract:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" (Rev. 3 is revision). The certificate number appears as \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\". I could put number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" and note revision elsewhere. Revision history is not present in this fragment (no revision table). The \"Rev. 3\" is part of the header. I might include it in number or as revision info. The schema has revision_history[] but no content of changes is present. I'll keep the number as \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" and maybe... Hmm. Actually, should I include \"Rev. 3\"? The rule says don't fabricate. The number with revision is \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\". Since revision_history requires date and changes which are absent, I'll set number to \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" — but then the Rev 3 info is lost. Alternatively, include revision \"3\"? The revision_history schema: revision (string), date, changes. No date/changes present. I could omit revision_history. But losing the revision seems bad. Hmm — maybe I can put number as \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" and add revision_history entry with revision \"3\" and null date/changes? The rule says don't fabricate; dates absent. I could include {\"revision\": \"3\"} only. That's not fabricating — it's present. I think that's acceptable. Or keep number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\". Given the instruction \"revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0)\", revision history entries presumably come from a revision table. I'll include number without \"Rev. 3\" and a revision_history minimal entry. Actually, safer: number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\", revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"3\"}]. Hmm, but is that \"Rev 3\" or is it part of certificate numbering? It's clearly revision 3. I'll include it.\n\n- scheme: \"A\" (from R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 — the A indicates Scheme A per OIML-CS).\n- page_total: 13 (from \"Page 10 of 13 pages\").\n- member_state: could infer \"Slovakia\" from SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY stamp and SK1 in number. The stamp says \"SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY\" and \"SLM\". The certificate number contains \"SK1\". I think member_state: \"Slovakia\" is justifiable — it's in the fragment via the stamp text. Yes, include \"Slovakia\".\n\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit.\n\n- Issuing authority: name \"Slovak Legal Metrology\" (SLM) appears in the stamp. But no address, person, etc. Should I include issuing_authority with just name? The stamp is an image description; \"SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY\" curved text. I could include name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\". Hmm, it's from a stamp description. It's reasonable. But cautious — maybe omit? The instruction: \"Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\" The stamp is present and indicates the issuing authority. I'll include issuing_authority with name only. Actually, I think it's fine and helpful.\n\n- Certified type: QALCOSONIC W1 water meter, designs A and B. The figure captions: \"Illustrative views of the water meters type QALCOSONIC W1 (design A)\" and \"(design B)\". Also markings observed on faceplates: \"AXIOMA\" (manufacturer name/trademark likely), \"M21\" (environmental class), \"T30\" (temperature class), \"QM15\" (Q3 = 15 m³/h? actually \"QM15\" likely means Q3=15 m³/h — but written as QM15... hmm, actually marking per R49 is Q3; \"QM15\" might be \"Q3 15\"? Whatever — it's what's legible), \"R160\" (R ratio = 160).\n\n- DN sizes: DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32, DN40 (from figure captions Fig. 2a/2b/2c).\n\nShould I create model_family? The cert covers design A and design B, and DN15–DN40 sizes. Since part 4 is a fragment, presumably earlier parts had the model tables. For THIS fragment, I can extract:\n- type_designations: [\"QALCOSONIC W1\"]\n- model_family: designs A and B → models: [{\"model_id\": \"design A\"}, {\"model_id\": \"design B\"}]? Hmm, and DN sizes. The figures show sealing for DN15/DN20, DN25/DN32, DN40. Those are size variants. But is it safe to enumerate models from figures only? The figure captions explicitly say design A and design B. I could include model_family with models design A and design B. But the earlier parts (not given) would have the full model matrix. The instruction says extract content present in this fragment only. I think including type_designations QALCOSONIC W1 is safe. Model family — I might include designs A/B as variants. Hmm.\n\nActually, be careful about over-extraction. The core deliverables from this fragment:\n\n1. certificate: number, scheme A, page_total 13, member_state Slovakia.\n2. characteristics.type_level? The marking requirements are normative text, not characteristic values. There's no numeric data with units to extract, except the observed faceplate markings (T30 → temperature class T30, i.e., 30°C; M21 → electromagnetic/environmental class; R160 → Q3/Q1 ratio 160; QM15 → flowrate Q3 = 15 m³/h?). Hmm — \"QM15\" — in some water meter markings, \"Q3 15\" means Q3 = 15 m³/h. The OCR reads \"QM15\". I shouldn't over-interpret. But R1