bserved markings into certified_type.description only, avoiding dubious characteristic extraction. But the schema wants structured characteristics when present. The faceplate markings are legitimate data. I'll include a few conservative ones:\n- temperature_class: \"T30\" (T30 = 30°C; I'll keep value \"T30\" string; could do value 30 unit °C with note that T-class. Safer: value \"T30\").\n- ratio_q3_over_q1: 160 (R160). unit null.\n- electromagnetic_environmental_class: \"M21\".\n- nominal_flowrate_marking: \"QM15\"? Hmm. I'll skip that one, or include as marking in description.\n\nActually \"QM15\" — hold on, could be \"Q3 15\" where Q3 has subscript. In R49 marking, it's \"Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 indicated as (R) followed by ratio value\". Markings \"Q3 15, R160\" → Q3=15 m³/h, R=160. OCR gave \"QM15\". So Q3 = 15 m³/h is quite likely. But per rule 8 (don't fabricate), I'll record it as observed: maybe characteristic \"flowrate_q3\" with value 15, unit \"m³/h\"? That's an interpretation of \"QM15\". Risky. Alternative: include in description: 'faceplate markings include \"QM15\" (flowrate Q3) and \"R160\"'. I'll put raw strings in description and optionally a type_level characteristic \"nominal_flowrate_q3\" with value 15, unit \"m³/h\"... Hmm.\n\nI'll be moderately conservative: include temperature_class \"T30\", electromagnetic class \"M21\", ratio R 160, and mention \"QM15\" in description without converting.\n\nHmm, actually wait — should M21 be electromagnetic class? Section 4(n) says \"electromagnetic environmental class\" shall be marked. So \"M21\" on the faceplate is likely that class (in R49, electromagnetic classes are E1, E2... but pressure/flow profile classes use M?). Actually R49-1/-2: environmental classes: climatic \"O/M\"? Let me recall: For water meters, R49 defines: temperature class (T30, T50...), environmental class (O, M, B for condensed/humid...), actually R49-2 Annex B: climatic and mechanical environment classes: Class O (unspecified), Class M (outdoor), Class B (indoor)... and electromagnetic classes E1, E2. Flow profile sensitivity classes (for electromagnetic...) Hmm. \"M21\" could be \"M2\" + \"1\"? or combined \"M2\" (electromagnetic?) Hmm. Actually, I recall \"M21\" markings on some meters = \"M2\" pressure sensitivity + something. Not sure. But the marking list includes: environmental classification, installation sensitivity class, electromagnetic environmental class, flow profile sensitivity class. So \"M21\" might combine \"M2\" and \"1\"... I'll just record electromagnetic_environmental_class? That may be wrong mapping. Safer to just record it as \"environmental_class_marking\": \"M21\"? Hmm.\n\nI think simplest: record characteristics:\n- \"temperature_class\": {value: \"T30\"}\n- \"electromagnetic_environmental_class\": {value: \"M21\"} — plausible given item (n). But \"M21\" could equally be the environmental classification (l) \"M2\" plus installation sensitivity \"1\"? I'll instead use a neutral attribute name: \"marking_m21\": no, ugly.\n\nAlternative approach: put all observed markings into certified_type.description verbatim and only extract unambiguous numeric ones. Actually, is even R160 → ratio 160 safe? \"R160\" per R49 marking item (g): \"ratio Q3/Q1 indicated as (R) followed by the ratio value\". So R160 ⇒ ratio value 160. Yes that's safe per the certificate's own text. I'll extract \"flow_ratio_q3_over_q1\" = 160. Hmm, actually T30 is temperature class: value \"T30\" or 30 °C. I'll do value \"T30\", and maybe note. Keep as string.\n\nFor \"QM15\": per item (g) flowrate Q3 — the marking shows Q3 value 15 (m³/h). OCR \"QM15\" — the M might be subscript 3 misread? \"Q₃15\"? Hmm, \"QM15\" could also be \"Q3 15\". I'll include in description only.\n\nDN sizes: Fig 2a sealing DN15 and DN20; Fig 2b DN25 and DN32; Fig 2c DN40. These are nominal sizes covered. Should I create model_family with DN models? Possibly the cert covers DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32, DN40. This fragment shows sealing figures for these DNs, implying they're covered types. I could add model_level characteristic \"nominal_size_dn\" with models DN15..DN40? But model_family.models requires model_id per variant. Hmm. The instruction says for THIS fragment extract what's present. I think adding model_family with designs A and B and maybe noting DN sizes is reasonable. But risk: earlier parts presumably already had the full model list; my fragment-only output is merged by the caller? Unknown. I'll include a modest model_family: models for designs A and B? And a type_level characteristic? Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe cleaner: certified_type.type_designations: [\"QALCOSONIC W1\"]; description mentioning designs A and B and DN sizes. characteristics.type_level: temperature_class, ratio, classes. And a config_level? No.\n\nLet me reconsider: Do I even include issuing_authority? The stamp description mentions SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY. I'll include name \"Slovak Legal Metrology\" (SLM). Also \"FK-P05-85\" and \"V04/24-12-02\" are internal form codes — could go... maybe into certificate.project_number? Not really. \"FK-P05-85\" is a form code of SLM; \"V04/24-12-02\" maybe version date (2004-12-02? or 2024-12-02?). Don't fabricate — leave out. Hmm, \"V04/24-12-02\" might be a form version dated 24-12-02 (2024-12-02). It's a footer artifact, not certificate data. Skip.\n\ndate_issued: not present in fragment. Skip.\n\ntest_reports: none. Skip.\n\nrecommendation: R49 is implied by number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\". Should I emit recommendation {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013?}? Hmm — R49/2013: the \"2013\" is the edition year of the recommendation version used (R49 2013 edition). Actually the OIML-CS cert numbering: \"R49/2013\" — the recommendation and its edition year? In OIML CS, certificate numbers often look like \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" where 2013 refers to... Typically it's \"R46/2015-FR1-...\" — the year indicates the edition of the Recommendation (2013 edition of R49). So recommendation.id \"R49\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\". Accuracy classes: none given in fragment. Include recommendation with id R49, edition 2013, scheme A. That seems legitimately derivable. Edition as int 2013 — the schema says edition (int). OK.\n\nShould I include the marking requirements as a list? The schema doesn't have such a section. I could add them in a matrix_tables? No. Maybe include in certified_type.description a summary. Or add a custom \"marking_requirements\" — no, stick to schema. Description text can capture the security measures and marking items briefly.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nRevision: revision_history [{\"revision\": \"3\"}]? The header shows \"Rev. 3\" repeatedly. Without date/changes. Rule 8: omit fields not present; the entry with only revision is fine. But maybe simpler: include in certificate number? The schema example: \"R60\" canonical id; number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\". I'll put number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" and revision_history with revision \"3\" and null-ish omitted fields. Actually maybe omit revision_history and just... no, losing info. I'll include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"3\", \"date\": null, \"changes\": null}]. Hmm, \"Empty strings are not values — use null or omit.\" I'll use omit: {\"revision\": \"3\"}.\n\nmember_state: \"Slovakia\" (from stamp + SK1 country code). I'll include.\n\npage_total: 13.\n\nscheme: \"A\".\n\nNow characteristics. type_level entries:\n\n1. temperature_class: {value: \"T30\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null} — from faceplate \"T30\".\n2. flow_ratio_r_q3_over_q1: {value: 160} — from \"R160\". Attribute name maybe \"ratio_q3_over_q1\".\n3. electromagnetic_environmental_class: {value: \"M21\"}? Uncertain mapping. Alternatively \"environmental_class\": \"M21\". Hmm. Given (l) environmental classification, (n) electromagnetic environmental class both exist... \"M21\" — I'd guess \"M2\" is the electromagnetic class? Actually in R49-1, the classes for influence quantities: \"electromagnetic environment classes E1, E2\" hmm no. Let me recall R49: Annex? R49-2: influences: humidity classes? The marking per R49-1 6.1: \"environment class\" letters: B (indoor fixed), O (outdoor), etc.? Honestly, \"M21\" might be a combined marking \"M2\" \"1\". I'll name it generically: \"environmental_class_marking\": \"M21\". Eh... that's odd too.\n\nAlternatively, put it in description only. I'll do that: description mentions legible markings \"CE\", \"M21\", \"T30\", \"QM15\", \"R160\". And extract T30 → temperature_class and R160 → ratio as characteristics (these two are safe per section 4 items i and g). M21 and QM15 keep in description raw.\n\nHmm, but arguably I could also add \"temperature_class\": value \"T30\" — fine.\n\nShould I add model_level for design A/B? No varying numeric attributes in this fragment. Skip model_level. But model_family — designs A and B are two variants shown. I'll include:\n\n\"model_family\": {\"family_name\": \"QALCOSONIC W1\", \"models\": [{\"model_id\": \"design A\"}, {\"model_id\": \"design B\"}]}\n\nHmm, family_name \"QALCOSONIC W1\" — is that a family name? It's the type name. The designs A and B are variants. I think it's acceptable. Or omit model_family entirely since no attributes vary here. The schema says include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant. The cert does cover design A and design B (as shown). I'll include it.\n\nAlso DN sizes: DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32, DN40 appear in figure captions. These could be additional model attributes. I could add model_level characteristic \"nominal_size_dn\"? But which designs have which DN? Unknown from fragment. The sealing figures group DN15/DN20, DN25/DN32, DN40 — suggesting these sizes exist. I'll mention in description: \"Sealing arrangements illustrated for DN15/DN20 (Fig. 2a), DN25/DN32 (Fig. 2b) and DN40 (Fig. 2c).\" That preserves data without fabricating model mappings.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"? The schema examples list categories like \"Measuring system\"... category for R49 is water meter. The example list doesn't include water meter but category is open-ended. Use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"QALCOSONIC W1\"]\n- module_designation: not applicable/not present → omit or \"Not applicable\". Not mentioned; omit.\n- description: \"Ultrasonic? \" — don't fabricate technology. Description: \"Water meters type QALCOSONIC W1, designs A and B. Fig. 1a/1b show illustrative views with faceplate markings including CE, M21, T30, QM15, R160 and the name AXIOMA. Section 4 lists required markings (manufacturer name/trademark, type name, unit m³, year of manufacture, serial number, flow direction arrow, Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 as (R), MAP, temperature class, pressure-loss class, latest replacement date, environmental classification, installation sensitivity class, electromagnetic environmental class, flow profile sensitivity class, national type approval sign). Section 5: sealed by wire+seal between upper cover and housing; casing imperceptibly closed; opening the sealed cover activates a safety button producing an error code on the display (Fig. 2). Sealing arrangements illustrated for DN15/DN20 (Fig. 2a), DN25/DN32 (Fig. 2b), DN40 (Fig. 2c).\"\n\nThat's long but fine.\n\nShould components be included? No sub-assemblies named. Skip.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\ntest_reports: none.\n\nNow certificate number: include \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\". project_number: \"SK1-23.02\"? The SK1-23.02 might be the certificate serial. Leave as part of number. project_number: omit.\n\nAlso \"V04/24-12-02\" — hmm, could be the form version \"V04\" dated 24-12-02. Skip.\n\nAlso could add footnotes: none.\n\nLet me also consider adding \"certificate.number\" with revision: The repeated header: \"OIML Certificate No. R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\". I'll do number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" and revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"3\"}].\n\nUnits: temperature_class value \"T30\" — unit_symbol null. ratio 160 — unit null (dimensionless ratio value).\n\nAttribute names: \"temperature_class\", \"ratio_q3_over_q1\". Good.\n\nmember_state: \"Slovakia\". Actually SK = Slovakia. Yes.\n\noiml_issuer_id: maybe \"SK1\"? In OIML-CS, issuer IDs are like \"NMi\", \"PTB\", \"SLM\"... \"SK1\" is the country code + sequence in the cert number, not issuer ID. Omit.\n\nissuing_authority: {\"name\": \"Slovak Legal Metrology\"} — from stamp. Should I add abbreviation \"SLM\"? name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\". Fine. website/email: omit.\n\nHmm — is including issuing_authority from a stamp description safe? The stamp is on every page; it's the national metrology authority of Slovakia. Yes, include name only.\n\nFinal JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 13,\n    \"member_state\": \"Slovakia\"\n  },\n  \"revision_history\": [\n    {\"revision\": \"3\"}\n  ],\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"QALCOSONIC W1\"],\n    \"description\": \"...\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"QALCOSONIC W1\",\n    \"models\": [{\"model_id\": \"design A\"}, {\"model_id\": \"design B\"}]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T30\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"ratio_q3_over_q1\": {\"value\": 160, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n    }\n  },\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\"\n  }\n}\n\nWait — the certificate-level scheme field: schema says certificate.scheme (A|B). From \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\": yes, \"A\".\n\nShould temperature_class have footnote markers? No.\n\nAlso consider \"nominal sizes\" as type_level characteristic? They vary per configuration (sealing differs), could be config_level: attribute \"sealing_arrangement_dn\"? Eh. I'll keep in description.\n\nOne more: \"QM15\" — maybe include